{"name":"Electric Eel","hex":"#88bbee","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"electric eel","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electrophorus electricus, a species of fish resembling an eel (though not related) that is capable of generating powerful electric shocks.","Any species of the genus Electrophorus."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/electric_eel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Electric eel","blocks":[{"text":"The electric eels are a genus, Electrophorus, of neotropical freshwater fish from South America in the family Gymnotidae, of which they are the only members of the subfamily Electrophorinae. They are known for their ability to stun their prey by generating electricity, delivering shocks at up to 860 volts. Their electrical capabilities were first studied in 1775, contributing to the invention of the electric battery in 1800."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Electric-eel.jpg/330px-Electric-eel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_eel"},{"title":"Electric Eel (roller coaster)","blocks":[{"text":"Electric Eel is a steel launched roller coaster at SeaWorld in San Diego, California. The coaster opened in 2018 as the park's then-tallest and fastest, until the opening of Emperor in 2022. The ride is loosely themed around the electric eel. An educational area and informational exhibit on electric eels can also be found by the attraction and near the queue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Electric_Eel_1.jpg/330px-Electric_Eel_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_Eel_(roller_coaster)"},{"title":"Eel","blocks":[{"text":"Eels are ray-finned fish belonging to the order Anguilliformes, which consists of eight suborders, 20 families, 164 genera, and about 1000 species. Eels undergo considerable development from the early larval stage to the eventual adult stage and are usually predators."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/New_Zealand_longfin_eel_%28Anguilla_dieffenbachii%29_Piha.jpg/330px-New_Zealand_longfin_eel_%28Anguilla_dieffenbachii%29_Piha.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eel"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Electric-eel.jpg/500px-Electric-eel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Electric-eel.jpg/1280px-Electric-eel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Electric-eel.jpg/500px-Electric-eel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AElectric-eel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/Anguilles_%C3%A9lectriques_3_esp%C3%A8ces_Nature_10_sept_2019_C._David_de_Santana_et_al.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/Anguilles_%C3%A9lectriques_3_esp%C3%A8ces_Nature_10_sept_2019_C._David_de_Santana_et_al.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Differences between the three species of electric eel, namely E. electricus, E. voltai, and E. varii[14]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnguilles_%C3%A9lectriques_3_esp%C3%A8ces_Nature_10_sept_2019_C._David_de_Santana_et_al.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Lateral_view_of_Electrophorus_electricus.png/500px-Lateral_view_of_Electrophorus_electricus.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wiki